1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,779
This screen is showing receipts, not pretending
to run a simulation. The 884 jobs were

2
00:00:09,779 --> 00:00:18,842
completed earlier and stored in a manifest—a
list of what ran and which files belong

3
00:00:18,842 --> 00:00:27,473
to each result. It names the fixed SWMM version,
the 220 plans, the four storms,

4
00:00:27,473 --> 00:00:36,212
and the numerical check for every job. The plans
were ordered in a repeatable way

5
00:00:36,212 --> 00:00:45,599
chosen in advance; machine learning did not
select this guided set. A local rerun would

6
00:00:45,599 --> 00:00:54,877
be different: the app would launch SWMM again,
show real progress, and compare the new

7
00:00:54,877 --> 00:01:03,832
answers with the stored ones. That control
appears only when the expected engine is

8
00:01:03,832 --> 00:01:12,787
actually available. It is not available in this
recording, so the app does not fake

9
00:01:12,787 --> 00:01:22,066
it. Replay lets us inspect what happened. Rerun
lets us test whether it happens again.

10
00:01:22,066 --> 00:01:28,000
Neither should be confused with work currently
running.
